GNU awk (gawk) for OpenVMS
==========================

This is GNU awk @VERSION@ built natively for OpenVMS @ARCH@, from the
unmodified GNU release (whose own OpenVMS port it uses) plus the patches in
the vms-awk project:

    https://github.com/issinoho/vms-awk

Kit version: @KITVERSION@.  GNU awk is free software under the GNU General
Public License, version 3 or later; see COPYING.  The manual page is in
GAWK.TXT (plain text) and gawk.1 (man page source).

Setting up
----------
The kit installs GAWK$STARTUP.COM into SYS$STARTUP and runs it once.  To
define GAWK$ROOT at every boot, the system manager adds this line to
SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:

    $ @SYS$STARTUP:GAWK$STARTUP.COM

Users then define the gawk and awk commands in LOGIN.COM:

    $ @GAWK$ROOT:[000000]GAWK$SETUP.COM

Things that differ from Unix
----------------------------
- Quote the program.  With the default PARSE_STYLE=TRADITIONAL, DCL
  upper-cases an unquoted command line and the C run-time library then
  lower-cases it.  Put programs and upper-case options in quotes, and
  double the quotes inside them:
      $ gawk "{ print $1 }" file.txt
      $ gawk "BEGIN { print ""hello"" }"
  or use SET PROCESS/PARSE_STYLE=EXTENDED.

- Redirection and pipes inside awk programs (print > "file",
  print | "command", "command" | getline) are handled by gawk's OpenVMS
  port; commands run with DCL.  gawk also accepts <, > and 2>&1 on its own
  command line.

- Exit status.  gawk exits with 0 on success, 1 or 2 on errors, or the
  value given to exit.  DCL sees these as C-facility condition values.

- Locales.  Choose one with a logical name, e.g. $ DEFINE LC_ALL "UTF8-20".
  IA64 ships only UTF8-20, which covers characters of up to three bytes.
